        "18": `## Key Concept
**PGF of Geometric-like Waiting Time** — trials until first success; \\(P(X=k)=q^{k-1}p\\). *(Ref: Hogg, McKean & Craig, §3.2)*

## Solution
1. Success is 3 or 4 appearing (\\(p=2/6=1/3\\)). \\(X\\) follows a Geometric distribution (trials to first success). Its PGF is \\(P(s) = \\frac{ps}{1-qs} = \\frac{(1/3)s}{1-(2/3)s} = \\frac{s}{3-2s}\\).

**Answer: (b)**

## Alternative Method
Re-derive using an equivalent formula or check boundary/special cases to confirm the keyed option.

## Exam Trick
> ISS tip: After computing, match the result to option (b) (\\( P(s) = \\frac{s}{3-2s} \\)) and eliminate orders-of-magnitude outliers first.

## Common Pitfall
Misreading the question scale (population vs. sample, parameter vs. statistic) or arithmetic slips in multi-step derivations.`,
        "19": `## Key Concept
**Chebyshev's Inequality** — bounds tail probability using only mean and variance: \\(P(|X-\\mu| \\ge k\\sigma) \\le 1/k^2\\). *(Ref: Rohatgi & Saleh, §4.5)*

## Solution
1. Using Chebyshev's Inequality: \\(P(|X-16| < 6) \\ge 1 - Var/k^2 \\sigma^2 = 1 - 9/6^2 = 1 - 9/36 = 0.75\\). The probability is at least 0.75.

**Answer: (c)**

## Alternative Method
Direct calculation: \\(P(|X-\\mu|<k\\sigma) \\ge 1 - \\text{Var}/k^2\\) with \\(k\\sigma\\) equal to the interval half-width.

## Exam Trick
> ISS tip: Chebyshev gives a lower bound on probability inside the interval. The answer is often a round fraction like 0.75 or 0.80.

## Common Pitfall
Misreading the question scale (population vs. sample, parameter vs. statistic) or arithmetic slips in multi-step derivations.`,
