        "34": `## Key Concept
**Normal Distribution** — bell-shaped distribution characterised by mean \\(\\mu\\) and variance \\(\\sigma^2\\) (Sample Mean Probability). *(Ref: Rohatgi & Saleh, An Introduction to Probability and Statistics, 2nd ed., §6.3)*

## Solution
1. \\(\\bar{X} \\sim N(0.1, 2.1^2/900) = N(0.1, 0.0049)\\). \\(SD = 0.07\\). \\(P(\\bar{X} < 0) = P(Z < (0 - 0.1)/0.07) = P(Z < -1.43)\\). Using table: \\(0.5 - 0.4236 = 0.0764\\).

**Answer: (a)**

## Alternative Method
Re-derive using an equivalent formula or check boundary/special cases to confirm the keyed option.

## Exam Trick
> ISS tip: After computing, match the result to option (a) (0.0764) and eliminate orders-of-magnitude outliers first.

## Common Pitfall
Misreading the question scale (population vs. sample, parameter vs. statistic) or arithmetic slips in multi-step derivations.`,
        "35": `## Key Concept
**T-distribution Construction** — fundamental probability and statistics concept for ISS Paper 1. *(Ref: Hogg, McKean & Craig, Introduction to Mathematical Statistics, 7th ed., §5.3)*

## Solution
1. A t-variable is \\(N(0,1) / \\sqrt{\\chi^2_k/k}\\). Here \\((X_1+X_2)/\\sqrt{2} \\sim N(0,1)\\) and \\(X_3^2+X_4^2+X_5^2 \\sim \\chi^2_3\\). The expression is \\(\\frac{(X_1+X_2)/\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{(X_3^2+X_4^2+X_5^2)/3}} = \\sqrt{3/2} \\frac{X_1+X_2}{\\sqrt{\\dots}}\\). Thus, \\(C = \\sqrt{3/2}\\).

**Answer: (b)**

## Alternative Method
Recall standard sampling distributions: \\(Z\\sim N(0,1)\\), \\(t=Z/\\sqrt{\\chi^2/k}\\), \\(F=\\chi^2_1/k_1 \\div \\chi^2_2/k_2\\).

## Exam Trick
> ISS tip: After computing, match the result to option (b) (\\( \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}} \\)) and eliminate orders-of-magnitude outliers first.

## Common Pitfall
Using \\(n\\) instead of \\(n-2\\) in the mean of \\(F\\), or confusing \\(F(m,n)\\) with \\(F(n,m)\\) when reciprocating.`,
        "36": `## Key Concept
**F-distribution** — ratio of independent chi-square variables (each divided by its df); used for variance ratios and ANOVA (Expectations). *(Ref: Hogg, McKean & Craig, Introduction to Mathematical Statistics, 7th ed., §5.3)*

## Solution
1. For \\(F(m, n)\\), \\(E(X) = n/(n-2)\\). Also \\(1/X \\sim F(n, m)\\), so \\(E(1/X) = m/(m-2)\\). The product is \\(\\frac{mn}{(m-2)(n-2)}\\).

**Answer: (c)**

## Alternative Method
Recall standard sampling distributions: \\(Z\\sim N(0,1)\\), \\(t=Z/\\sqrt{\\chi^2/k}\\), \\(F=\\chi^2_1/k_1 \\div \\chi^2_2/k_2\\).

## Exam Trick
> ISS tip: For \\(F(1,1)\\), numerator and denominator must be independent \\(\\chi^2_1\\) variables — equal population variances are essential when differencing normal samples.

## Common Pitfall
Using \\(n\\) instead of \\(n-2\\) in the mean of \\(F\\), or confusing \\(F(m,n)\\) with \\(F(n,m)\\) when reciprocating.`,
